  "query_preview": "..The upper pattern this morning is defined by a \ntrough of low pressure extending along the West Coast and the \nsubtropical high positioned over the southeastern states. The \ndeep moist airmass that has been blanketing Southeast Arizona \nwill begin to erode today as drier air (PWATs 1.00-1.25 inches \nseen on the GOES precipitable water imagery) across northern \nSonora tracks into our neck of the woods behind the southwesterly\nflow. This should result in a significant downturn in thunderstorm\nactivity with a low-grade monsoon day on tap. The big question,\nis how much we can dry out today even with the H7 theta-e ridge\nnudging east into NM. Given how saturated the soils are across \nthe entire forecast area, this should slow the anticipated \ndrying with eastern areas holding on their moisture into the \nafternoon. There should be enough moisture around today for \nisolated thunderstorms mainly over the higher terrain but may \nstruggle to support themselves as they move across the valleys.\nThe drier air in the lower-to-mid levels will increase the \npotential that downdrafts will result in gusty and erratic \noutflows today but this impact should remain localized. There is\nstill enough moisture to support brief heavy rainfall...which \nmay end-up being problematic in those areas with existing \nsaturated soils from the past few days.\n\nOtherwise, the big story will be the increasing heat early next\nweek. The upper-high center across the SERN states is expected \nto move west the next few days. In fact, the high should be \nover ERN TX by 16/00Z Saturday afternoon, WRN TX by 17/00Z \nSunday afternoon, then overhead 18/06Z Monday evening...finally\nmaking its way to southern California by Tuesday. The model \n850-700mb thicknesses increase to around 1690m next Monday and \nTuesday which correlates to afternoon high temperatures near \n106 degrees in Tucson. This will elevate the overall HeatRisk \nto Major for the lower deserts and Extreme for some locales \nincluding Tucson and Safford. An Extreme Heat Watch is in \neffect Monday through Wednesday for a majority of Pima county, \nPinal county, southern Graham and Greenlee counties. The upper \nridge then shifts into the Great Basin late next week and next \nweekend, which should open us back-up for a more active thunderstorm\npattern.",
